## Solution

We start from the equilibrium equation:
$$\left(z+\frac{T}{z}\right)^{2}=1, \qquad T=\sqrt{1347}.$$

Taking square roots gives two cases:

**Case 1:** $z+\dfrac{T}{z}=1 \;\Longrightarrow\; z^{2}-z+T=0$, so
$$z=\frac{1\pm\sqrt{1-4T}}{2}.$$

**Case 2:** $z+\dfrac{T}{z}=-1 \;\Longrightarrow\; z^{2}+z+T=0$, so
$$z=\frac{-1\pm\sqrt{1-4T}}{2}.$$

Since $T=\sqrt{1347}>0$ is real and $4T\gg 1$, the discriminant $1-4T<0$. Set
$$D=4T-1=4\sqrt{1347}-1>0,\qquad \sqrt{1-4T}=i\sqrt{D}.$$

The four roots are therefore
$$z_{1}=\tfrac{1+i\sqrt{D}}{2},\quad z_{2}=\tfrac{1-i\sqrt{D}}{2},\quad z_{3}=\tfrac{-1+i\sqrt{D}}{2},\quad z_{4}=\tfrac{-1-i\sqrt{D}}{2}.$$

As points in the plane $(\Re z,\,\Im z)$:

| point | $\Re z$ | $\Im z$ |
|---|---|---|
| $z_1$ | $+\tfrac12$ | $+\tfrac{\sqrt D}{2}$ |
| $z_2$ | $+\tfrac12$ | $-\tfrac{\sqrt D}{2}$ |
| $z_3$ | $-\tfrac12$ | $+\tfrac{\sqrt D}{2}$ |
| $z_4$ | $-\tfrac12$ | $-\tfrac{\sqrt D}{2}$ |

These are the four corners of an axis-aligned **rectangle**:
- horizontal extent: from $-\tfrac12$ to $+\tfrac12$, i.e. **width** $=1$,
- vertical extent: from $-\tfrac{\sqrt D}{2}$ to $+\tfrac{\sqrt D}{2}$, i.e. **height** $=\sqrt D$.

Hence the area is
$$\boxed{\text{Area}=1\cdot\sqrt{D}=\sqrt{\,4\sqrt{1347}-1\,}}.$$

